  "final_text": "All four steps are done, and I checked the results by reading the dashboard and the rule back.\n\n1. **Data view:** 'Kibana Sample Data Flights' (id `d3d7af60-4c81-11e8-b3d7-01146121b73d`, index `kibana_sample_data_flights`) has `timestamp` as its time field. Its other fields include `Carrier`, `Cancelled`, `FlightDelay`, `Origin`, `Dest`, `AvgTicketPrice` and `DistanceKilometers`.\n2. **Dashboard:** 'S4-79dbb7b2 Flights' (id `0fd28b9d-5fb9-4c11-bdcf-06b4fb935a42`) has two panels. One is a line chart of flight counts over time. The other is a bar chart of flight counts by `Carrier`, limited to the top 10.\n3. **Alert rule:** 'S4-79dbb7b2 Cancelled flights' (id `ad1390f7-348b-43a3-99fe-f98bb751827c`) is an Elasticsearch query rule. It runs every minute and counts flights with `Cancelled: true` over a 5-minute window. It fires when the count is above 10. It was created enabled, so I didn't need to enable it afterwards.\n4. **Panel edit:** The carrier panel's title is now 'Flights by carrier'. I sent the whole dashboard definition in the update, and the read-back shows both panels with the new title. The rule reads back as enabled.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
